es and American medicine. JAMA. 1995;273:1521&#x02013;7. <pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">7739079</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="CR27"><label>27.</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Fink</surname><given-names>KS</given-names></name><name><surname>Phillips</surname><given-names>RL</given-names></name><name><surname>Fryer</surname><given-names>GE</given-names></name><name><surname>Koehn</surname><given-names>N</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>International medical graduates and the primary care workforce for rural underserved areas</article-title><source>Health Aff</source><year>2003</year><volume>22</volume><fpage>255</fpage><lpage>262</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1377/hlthaff.22.2.255</pub-id></citation><citation citation-type="display-unstructured">Fink KS, Phillips RL, Jr., Fryer GE, Koehn N. International medical graduates and the primary care workforce for rural underserved areas. Health Aff. 2003;22:255&#x02013;62. </citation></ref><ref id="CR28"><label>28.</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Mullan</surname><given-names>F</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>The metrics of the physician brain drain</article-title><source>N Engl J Med</source><year>2005</year><volume>353</volume><fpage>1810</fpage><lpage>1818</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1056/NEJMsa050004</pub-id></citation><citation citation-type="display-unstructured">Mullan F. The metrics of the physician brain drain. N Engl J Med. 2005;353:1810&#x02013;8. <pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16251537</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="CR29"><label>29.</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Hallock</surname><given-names>JA</given-names></name><name><surname>Seeling</surname><given-names>SS</given-names></name><name><surname>Norcini</surname><given-names>JJ</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>The international medical graduate pipeline</article-title><source>Health Aff</source><year>2003</year><volume>22</volume><fpage>94</fpage><lpage>96</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1377/hlthaff.22.4.94</pub-id></citation><citation citation-type="display-unstructured">Hallock JA, Seeling SS, Norcini JJ. The international medical graduate pipeline. Health Aff. 2003;22:94&#x02013;6. </citation></ref><ref id="CR30"><label>30.</label><citation citation-type="other"><bold>Regets MC.</bold> Research and Policy Issues in High-skilled International Migration: a Perspective with Data from the United States. Arlington VG: National Science Foundation; 2001. Discussion paper 366.</citation></ref><ref id="CR31"><label>31.</label><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Adams</surname><given-names>O</given-names></name><name><surname>Kinnon</surname><given-names>C</given-names></name></person-group><source>A Public Health Perspective. International Trade in Health Services: a Developmental Perspective</source><year>1998</year><publisher-loc>Geneva</publisher-loc><publisher-name>World Health Organization</publisher-name></citation><citation citation-type="display-unstructured">Adams O, Kinnon C. A Public Health Perspective. International Trade in Health Services: a Developmental Perspective. Geneva: World Health Organization; 1998. </citation></ref></ref-list></back></article>